# Keep the Android cpusets from collapsing onto a single core.
#
# Rationale and measurements are in rootdir/m95-cpuset.sh; the short version is
# that the generic init.rc writes fixed cpu masks, the kernel intersects them
# with whatever MTK's hotplug governor has online, and on this deca-core the
# result was every cpuset holding exactly cpu4.
#
# Resident, not oneshot. A single pass at `on boot` was tried first and is not
# enough: on the 2026-08-08 build the service ran to completion and the sets
# were still down to cpu4 a minute later, because the generic init.rc writes
# its own fixed masks around the same moment and hotplug keeps pruning after
# that. Triggering on sys.boot_completed is no answer either - while anything
# earlier in the boot is broken, that property never arrives.
#
# The script therefore re-syncs every five seconds and never returns. That
# interval comes from the measured decay - cores were pruned out of a freshly
# written set within eight seconds. See the header of m95-cpuset.sh for why no
# userspace loop can do better than mitigate this, and what the real fix is.

service m95_cpuset /vendor/bin/sh /vendor/bin/m95-cpuset.sh
    # Explicit domain: this service runs /system/bin/sh, and a file-context
    # transition cannot serve three scripts that share one executable.
    seclabel u:r:m95_cpuset:s0
    class core
    user root
    group root
    disabled

on boot
    start m95_cpuset
